**Read:** [Systems Engineering Handbook — NASA](../../../engineering/mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/sources/Systems-Engineering-Handbook-NASA.lingua#r_7ZQFZ9E1NAVG27SE2R5S24PDBQ), chapter 5's verification methods, analysis and reporting. Revisit [Validate the surface method for the decision it supports](../surface-measurement/Validate-the-surface-method-for-the-decision-it-supports.lingua#r_7PSDBG99EP9AFB7KGM3W5HN8S2).

Inspection, analysis, demonstration and measurement support different parts of an argument. A physical inspection can establish a connection; a model can predict a consequence; a measurement can challenge the model under particular conditions. Combining them is useful when their assumptions and dependencies are visible.

For each requirement, identify the observation, configuration, comparison rule and unresolved uncertainty. A simulation validated against one operating state needs additional justification before extrapolation. Reusing a supplier's result requires knowing what specimen and boundary it represents.

Two apparently independent reports may share the same calibration, model or unverified assumption. Agreement then supplies less new information than two independent observations. End-to-end evidence tests interactions that separate component checks can miss.

**Work:** Map an airflow, a surface-loading and a functional requirement to evidence for a benign enclosure. Include one shared reference error and one interface failure. Design an observation that can distinguish a correct collection of components from an incorrectly integrated system.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control — from familiar objects to controlled processes](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
